<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The update feed for Context for Claude.

  This file IS the feed. It is served straight out of the repository over
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/..., which is the URL in Resources/Info.plist's SUFeedURL, and
  publishing an update is a commit to this file. That is the whole hosting story, and it is
  deliberately not the one desktop/macos uses.

  Omi's feed is generated by a FastAPI route (backend/routers/updates.py) that reads GitHub Releases
  through Firestore and Redis. That design earns its keep there: two channels, a beta identity with
  its own enclosures, a daily release cadence, and a server-side kill switch worth having when a bad
  build is already on ten thousand machines. None of it applies here. Borrowing it would tie a
  standalone app's ability to ship a fix to a production backend deploy it otherwise has nothing to
  do with, and this app exists precisely to not be part of that one.

  What this arrangement buys instead: the signature below is reviewable in the pull request that
  publishes it, next to the version it signs. A tampered feed is a visible commit. There is no
  service to be down, and no environment where the app is configured differently from what is in git.

  What it costs, so the next person is not surprised: raw.githubusercontent.com has no SLA and a
  roughly 5 minute CDN TTL, there is no server-side kill switch (pulling a bad release means another
  commit), and moving the feed elsewhere later needs a new build, because SUFeedURL is read from the
  bundle. See docs/releasing.md.

  There are no item elements yet because nothing has been released yet. Sparkle reads an empty
  channel as "no update available", which is the correct answer. The first release adds the first
  item, in the shape below; scripts/package-dmg.sh prints the two values that go in the enclosure,
  and docs/releasing.md is the full procedure.

      <item>
        <title>1.1.0</title>
        <sparkle:version>2</sparkle:version>
        <sparkle:shortVersionString>1.1.0</sparkle:shortVersionString>
        <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>14.4</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
        <pubDate>Mon, 03 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
        <description><![CDATA[ <ul><li>What changed, in the user's words.</li></ul> ]]></description>
        <enclosure
          url="https://github.com/BasedHardware/omi/releases/download/context-for-claude-v1.1.0/ContextForClaude-1.1.0.zip"
          length="00000000"
          type="application/octet-stream"
          sparkle:edSignature="the string sign_update printed" />
      </item>

  sparkle:version is CFBundleVersion and is what Sparkle compares; sparkle:shortVersionString is
  CFBundleShortVersionString and is only what it displays. A build number that does not increase is
  an update nobody receives.
-->
<rss version="2.0" xmlns:sparkle="http://www.andymatuschak.org/xml-namespaces/sparkle">
  <channel>
    <title>Context for Claude</title>
    <description>Updates for Context for Claude, the ambient context app for Claude on macOS.</description>
    <language>en</language>
  </channel>
</rss>
